   "passage": "**[1]** Not long ago, paying by phone at a traditional market would have seemed absurd. Today, a printed QR code hangs beside the scales at countless vegetable stalls. Traders who once kept their takings in a tin now watch payments arrive on a screen.\n\n**[2]** The appeal is easy to see. Sellers no longer need to hunt for small change, and they are less exposed to counterfeit banknotes. Because every sale is recorded, a trader can also show a bank a reliable history of income, which makes it easier to obtain a loan. For many small businesses, this record is their first formal proof that they can be trusted to repay.\n\n**[3]** Yet adoption has been uneven. Older traders are often reluctant to rely on a device they do not fully understand, and some distrust money they cannot hold. Others complain that funds sometimes take a day to reach their accounts, whereas cash is available at once. In areas where the signal is weak, a payment may fail altogether, leaving buyer and seller equally frustrated.\n\n**[4]** Supporters argue that these obstacles are temporary. They point out that similar doubts greeted bank cards a generation ago. Still, most agree that cash will linger in the markets for years, simply because it works when nothing else does.",
